WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:03.560
Abraham: Let’s discuss the
possible motives for cyber crime.

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.960
The motives for cyber crime are
similar to those for crime in

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.599
general only they involve the
benefits of 'online anonymity'.

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:15.959
Cyber crime can be on more of a 'small'
scale where it can impact an individual

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.850
directly such as cyber bullying or phone
hacking or a small company that has

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:22.689
their whole computer system taken over.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:28.739
This may be to gain control of their data,
holding them to ransom or adding malware.

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:32.709
However, it can also be on a large
scale where governments and large

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.410
organisations can be affected by
state sponsored attacks on their

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.510
security with world-wide repercussions.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.200
The motivation for cyber criminals
can be wide-ranging, for example

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.929
those linked to ransomware, phishing,
internet and financial fraud, password

00:00:47.929 --> 00:00:52.950
cracking, hacking and malware are
mainly motivated by financial gain but

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:58.570
can also be associated with pranks,
activism, cyber theft and espionage.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:04.760
Cyber crimes against people such as cyber
stalking, bullying, trolling and revenge

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:10.130
pornography are likely to be motivated by
hatred or to intimidate or inflict harm.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:14.500
Additionally, some cyber crime is
committed solely ‘because they can’!

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:18.920
Often criminals will hack a website or
computer just to prove to other hackers

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.240
that they can and to cause mayhem.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.560
These are usually younger people
just getting into hacking with

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:25.869
spare time on their hands.

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:30.429
An example of this is the hacking
group LulzSec, who would often breach

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.289
news outlets like The Sun and The
Times and create fake news stories.

